   (verify
    (smoke "/todos everything + from-notes + inbox")
    (smoke "cycle note TODO write-back")
    (smoke "create list and add item"))

  )
  (subsection kanban
   (doc "Board view over todos: one todo-list as a board with its own columns (per-list state set), cards draggable via tree actions")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|2026-12 campaign 9.0: flipped to implemented. BORGE-1786336755
        closed - the per-list state-cycle threading is live: todo/item/cycle
        + todo/cycle read the item's list's OWN columns (single source of
        truth todo/state/columns-for-list, which falls back to
        todo/state/list) and the detail <select> uses list-aware
        todo/state/options-for-list, so a custom-columns list cycles exactly
        its columns in /todos, the todo detail, and /kanban alike. There are
        no duplicates - /todos detail and /kanban read the same function.|)))
   (implements libs/kanban/lib.pp libs/todo/lib.pp)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A kanban is a VIEW over the existing todo model, not a
         new data type. A board IS one todo-list; its columns ARE
         that list's OWN state set (a `columns` field on the
         todo-list datum); its cards ARE that list's todo-items.
         No kanban-card type, no kanban-board type. This is the
         first product consumer of the tree prim family as a
         DATA API (one-tree.tree-datum-prims): every mutation is
         a tree action over a datum node (the list for column
         config, the cards for moves), not a create/update call.

         PER-LIST COLUMNS, NOT A GLOBAL STATE LIST. The todo
         package ships a default state list (todo/state/list: "",
         TODO, DONE, WAITING, CANCELLED). A list that does not
         set its own `columns` falls back to that default — so
         existing /todos and existing lists are unchanged. A
         list that sets `columns` overrides the default for
         itself: the board renders exactly those columns, in
         that order, and a card's `state` is one of them. This
         makes the todo state model per-list configurable instead
         of a single fixed global — the same mechanism answers
         "set your own columns" and "set your own todo state":
         edit the list's `columns` field. (The default list stays
         as the fallback and the seed for new lists; it is not
         deleted.)

         BOARD = ONE TODO-LIST. The /kanban route takes a list_id
         (defaults to the Inbox). A list-picker switches boards.
         Adding a card adds a todo-item to the list (create, the
         id-generating convenience); the board re-reads.

         CARDS = todo-items of the list, grouped by state. Read
         via tree/list over data.u.<user>.todo-item.* plus a
         list_id filter, OR via the todo package's existing
         todo/items-for-list helper composed with tree/list.
         Each card shows title + due; click opens the existing
         todo detail. Cards whose `state` is not in the board's
         current columns render in a trailing "other" bucket so a
         removed/renamed column never silently hides cards.

         MUTATIONS ARE TREE ACTIONS (the point of this
         subsection + the prerequisite one-tree fix). The list
         datum and each card datum are tree_nodes; every mutation
         is tree/get + dict-set* + tree/upsert at the same path:
           - MOVE CARD BETWEEN COLUMNS (drop on a state column):
             tree/get the card's datum node, dict-get its body,
             dict-set* the `state` field to the target column
             name and `position` to the drop index, tree/upsert
             the new body at the SAME path. Goes through the
             reverse mirror so (get 'todo-item id) and
             (rows 'todo-item) see the new state, exactly as if
             update had written it.
           - REORDER WITHIN A COLUMN (drop between two cards):
             same tree/get + dict-set* `position` per moved
             card; wrap the N affected cards in with-transaction
             so the reorder is atomic and no two cards share a
             position mid-drop.
           - ADD / REMOVE A COLUMN: tree/get the LIST datum,
             dict-set* its `columns` field to the new array,
             tree/upsert the list at the same path. Removing a
             column does NOT delete its cards (they land in the
             "other" bucket until re-assigned).
           - RENAME A COLUMN: tree/get the list, dict-set* the
             new name into `columns`, AND cascade — in one
             with-transaction, tree/upsert every card of that
             list whose `state` is the old name with the new
             name. Rename is all-or-nothing across list + cards.
           - No re-parenting / tree/move in v1: a card's list is
             a field (list_id), not a path segment, so moving a
             card to another board is a field update, not a path
             change. tree/move is available per one-tree.tree-
             datum-prims but unused here.

         UI / HTMX SHAPE:
           - GET /kanban?list=<id> renders html/page with a flex
             row of columns. Each column is a <section> with an
             hx-post drop target; each card is a draggable
             article with a tiny dragstart/drop JS shim that
             encodes the dragged card id + drop position into
             hidden form fields, then submits the column's
             hx-post. Same POST-to-one-route + hx-request?
             partial-vs-redirect pattern as /todos.
           - A column header has inline add/rename/remove
             controls (small forms, same hx-post pattern); the
             board footer has an "add column" form.
           - POST /kanban dispatches on action: move (card to
             column + position), add-column, rename-column,
             remove-column. Each performs the tree action(s)
             above and returns the refreshed board partial via
             render-html when hx-request?, else redirects.
           - No JS framework, no new asset pipeline — the shim is
             a small <script> in the page, same style as the
             outline keyboard-nav script (must pass scripts-
             check per notes-ui-contract).
           - Accessibility: each card reachable by keyboard; move
             via a per-card up/down/left/right control as a
             no-DnD fallback (the DnD shim is progressive
             enhancement, not the only path).

         RELATION TO /todos: the /todos page keeps its list/detail
         smart-view UX; /kanban is a sibling route over the same
         rows. Both read/write the same todo-item datum nodes; no
         duplication, no sync. Cycling state from /todos updates
         the kanban and vice versa because they share the store.
         A list with custom columns shows those same states as
         the /todos state cycle options for that list (the state
         machine reads the list's `columns` when present, else
         the global default).

         NON-GOALS (v1):
           - No WIP limits, no swimlanes, no per-user assignment
             columns, no card cover images.
           - No multi-list board (board = exactly one list).
           - No tree/move re-parenting of cards across types.
           - No per-USER state set (columns live on the LIST, not
             the user; a user's different lists may differ).
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "GET /kanban?list=<inbox> renders the inbox's items grouped into columns by the list's columns (or the default state list when the list has none), empty columns included")
    (smoke "a list with its own columns field renders exactly those columns; /todos for that list cycles only those states")
    (smoke "drag a card to another column (POST action=move) → the todo-item's state field changes via tree/upsert; /todos and /kanban both reflect it")
    (smoke "add / remove / rename a column → the list's columns field updates via tree/upsert; rename cascades to cards in one with-transaction")
    (smoke "reorder within a column → positions renumber atomically (with-transaction); no two cards share a position")
    (smoke "keyboard no-DnD fallback moves a card left/right between columns")))
  (subsection search-modal
   (doc "Typeahead find modal: one server search feeds open-note and refile; flat grouped results down to header level")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: notes/search + notes/ui/search-partial; route /partials/notes-search; live GET ?q=agents → 200 grouped hits.|) *)
   (implements libs/notes/lib.pp)
   (verify
    (smoke "GET /partials/notes-search?q=… returns grouped result HTML (docs + header rows with › breadcrumbs)")
    (smoke "results arrive without enumerating the garden in Nopales (filter happens in Postgres)")
    (smoke "modal opens via keyboard shortcut; arrows navigate; Enter opens chosen row")
    (smoke "empty query and no-match queries render stable non-error markup")
    (smoke "refile entry point uses the same typeahead; choosing a row refiles the selected node")
    (smoke "node-targeted rows refile under that heading; doc rows refile at root"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PRODUCT:
           One find-box is the "open note" interface AND the
           "refile destination" interface. Cmd/Ctrl-K opens a modal
           with a single input; typing shows results immediately.
           This subsection resolves priority-7 "search polish".

         INTERACTION MODEL (locked: flat, grouped):
           Every keystroke re-queries the server; the result list is
           ONE flat stream grouped under doc headings, header-level
           matches indented beneath their doc with a breadcrumb path:
             JOURNAL
               2026 › July 12 session
             TODO
               Projects › pricklypear
           Arrow keys move, Enter activates. NOT a two-tier
           drill-down (→/← to enter docs): slower when you already
           know the header word, and refile wants doc+node targets
           in one list anyway.

         RESULT ROW:
           (doc-slug doc-title node-id|() header-path matched-field)
           node-id () = doc-level hit (title/tags match).
           header-path = "Gran › Parent › Header" for tree cutover
           storage; pre-cutover (fields.outline JSON blob) only
           doc-level rows are emitted — header granularity ARRIVES
           WITH the tree cutover, it is not retrofitted onto the
           blob. matched-field lets the UI badge title vs body vs
           header hits.

         SEARCH STRATEGY (v1 — tree-backed):
           Notes are tree-backed (notes/tree/enabled? #t), so the
           search runs over tree_nodes, NOT data_rows. It uses the
           tree/search-text prim (BORGE-1785605474): one Postgres
           ILIKE query over tree_nodes scoped to the garden root
           (path <@ $prefix::ltree) for note rows (title/tags/slug)
           plus a second query for heading rows (title/body with a
           ›-joined header_path breadcrumb), merged + deduped by
           slug in OCaml, capped (≤20 rows). Empty/no-match queries
           return (); garden-wide Nopales enumeration is FORBIDDEN
           on this path (BORGE-1785603757: ~3k notes × eager
           enumeration blew the 100k step budget) — filtering
           happens in Postgres. Prim signature:
             (tree/search-text needle [limit])         — garden root
             (tree/search-text prefix needle [limit])  — explicit path
           Note: data_rows find-text (fields->>f ILIKE, crud.ml) is
           NOT used — it would miss the tree garden. Doc-level rows
           carry empty node_id; header rows include header_path +
           node_id (refile to a header parents under that node).
           Ranking v1 in OCaml: exact-slug first, title hits, then
           header, then body. Fuzzy/typo tolerance (pg_trgm) and
           ranked FTS (tsvector, cf. dabar.search-fts) are later
           upgrades that swap the query behind the SAME partial —
           the modal does not change (the prim signature is the
           seam). Track under .borge-bugs when demanded by use; not
           spec'd here.

         NON-GOALS (v1):
           - No load-the-whole-tree-into-JS client search
           - No tsvector/trgm migration (see above)
           - No cross-type global search (notes only; a global
             launcher is a product-experience question, later)
           - No search inside archived/hidden docs unless the
             current notes/doc/list filter already shows them

         UI / HTMX SHAPE:
           Follows the details-popover precedent of
           notes/ui/refile-modal (BORGE-1785603757) — no JS
           framework, no new asset pipeline. Modal is a <details>
           (or <dialog> if focus trapping proves needed) opened by
           clicking "find" or Cmd/Ctrl-K (tiny script, same style
           as the existing outline keyboard-nav script). Input has
             hx-get="/partials/notes-search"
             hx-trigger="input changed delay:200ms"
             hx-target on the results container.
           Arrow/Enter handling extends that script; htmx
           `htmx:afterSwap` re-focuses the first result.

         ROUTES (all private, current branch):
           GET /partials/notes-search?q=…         → grouped result HTML
           GET /partials/notes-search?q=…&mode=refile
             → same list, rows refile the selected outline node
               (POST action=refile with dst_slug + optional
                dst_node_id) instead of navigating.
           Open-note activation navigates to /notes?slug=…
           (&node=… for header rows when the tree cutover lands).

         REFILE CONVERGENCE:
           notes/ui/refile-modal's raw dst_slug text input gains a
           mode=refile typeahead against the same partial (exact
           slug entry keeps working — no regression for agents /
           keyboard-only users who know the slug). Refile to a
           header target parents the subtree under that node;
           refile to a doc row parents at root (current behavior).
           The existing POST /notes action=refile handler accepts
           an optional dst_node_id.

         BUDGET INVARIANT:
           /notes?slug=… page render must stay O(1) in garden size:
           the modal ships collapsed markup only; all searching is
           on-demand partials. Any future caching (e.g. a per-branch
           doc-index value) is optional and must be invalidated
           conservatively; premature caching is a non-goal.
     |))
   )
  )

 (section note-visibility
  (doc "Per-note vis (private | users | public, absent reads private) plus the read-only cross-garden route /notes/<user>/<slug>; the kernel prims enforce the floor, the lib stamps and renders")
  (status partial)
  (verify
   (build "dune build image")
   (smoke "owner reads own notes in own garden with vis absent (no migration)")
   (smoke "other signed-in user and anonymous: find-by in the owner's garden returns nil for vis private; tree/get into the garden denies")
   (smoke "vis users: other signed-in user reads via /notes/<owner>/<slug>; anonymous still denied")
   (smoke "vis public: anonymous visitor renders the read-only page with the owner breadcrumb")
   (smoke "upsert stamps vis private on create, preserves it on update; set-vis persists; POST action=set-vis")
   (smoke "/notes?slug=… and POST /notes unchanged; /notes/<user> and deeper shapes 400")
   (smoke "scripts/smoke_note_vis.sh end-to-end (two identities + anonymous)")
   )
  (details
   (doc (|
        MODEL (spec_note_visibility, 2026-09-18):
          Every note body carries vis: private | users | public.
          Absent reads private, so existing notes need no migration.
          vis only ever WIDENS: garden and space membership stay the
          floor. Writes are unchanged (vis never grants write access).

        KERNEL ENFORCEMENT (the lib never decides visibility):
          - tree/find-by: every Lisp call carries the caller as viewer.
            The SQL gains one clause: a row OUTSIDE the caller's garden
            floor (data.note.u.<caller>) must carry vis public, or
            users with a signed-in caller. prefix remains a plain
            scope filter; the cross-garden page passes the target
            garden as prefix and the kernel does the rest.
          - tree/get, tree/get-uuid, tree/get-many: a read landing in
            another user's note garden raises "tree read: denied:
            ... (vis ...)" unless the body vis allows the caller;
            anonymous callers only ever match public. Kernel-internal
            callers (slug uniqueness, sync internals) pass viewer=None
            and stay ungated.
          - NOT yet gated (pre-existing surfaces, next): tree/list,
            tree/children-of, tree/subtree, tree/list-kind,
            tree/note-index, tree/search-text. The global doc index
            still lists across gardens; P2 tags each entry with owner
            + vis before it links anywhere.

        LIB (libs/notes/lib.pp):
          - notes/doc/upsert-in stamps vis: create gets private,
            update keeps the row's vis; the legacy data_rows path
            stamps the same via dict-set*.
          - notes/doc/set-vis rebuilds the note body with vis
            (move-safe); the note page gets a small selector next to
            delete; POST action=set-vis persists it.
          - notes/page branches on cross-user / cross-slug params:
            the kernel router decodes the /notes/<user>/<slug>
            segments (Packages.name_decode, /edit/ convention) and
            injects them, so one page body serves /notes and the
            cross-garden shape. show-cross renders read-only (outline
            without actions, no toolbar, no scripts) with the owner
            breadcrumb; absent and not-visible both render the plain
            Missing page, so the route never leaks existence.
      |))
  )

 )
)
